        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.165,
            0.718,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "5 0 \\mathrm { t h }   \\mathrm { o b s e r v a t o n }   \\mathrm { i s }   2 8 \\qquad \\mathrm { ( W h y ? ) }",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.192,
            0.605,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "51st observation is 29"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.237,
            0.135,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.291,
            0.225,
            0.552,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Media} = \\frac{28 + 29}{2} = 28.5",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.276,
            0.902,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "**Remark :** The part of Table 13.11 consisting Column 1 and Column 3 is known as Cumulative Frequency Table. The median marks 28.5 conveys the information that about 50% students obtained marks less than 28.5 and another 50% students obtained marks more than 28.5."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.364,
            0.902,
            0.404
          ],
          "content": "Now, let us see how to obtain the median of grouped data, through the following situation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.411,
            0.901,
            0.452
          ],
          "content": "Consider a grouped frequency distribution of marks obtained, out of 100, by 53 students, in a certain examination, as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.23,
            0.493,
            0.815,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": "| Marks | Number of students |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0 - 10 | 5 |\n| 10 - 20 | 3 |\n| 20 - 30 | 4 |\n| 30 - 40 | 3 |\n| 40 - 50 | 3 |\n| 50 - 60 | 4 |\n| 60 - 70 | 7 |\n| 70 - 80 | 9 |\n| 80 - 90 | 7 |\n| 90 - 100 | 8 |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.435,
                0.471,
                0.565,
                0.489
              ],
              "content": "Table 13.12"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.097,
                0.846,
                0.671,
                0.866
              ],
              "content": "From the table above, try to answer the following questions:"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.147,
                0.871,
                0.893,
                0.892
              ],
              "content": "How many students have scored marks less than 10? The answer is clearly 5."
            }
          ],
